  <h1>Surviving your first crit</h1>
  <p class="lead muted">The crit is architecture education&rsquo;s defining ritual — daunting the first time, but a skill you can absolutely learn.</p>

  <h2>What a crit actually is</h2>
  <p>A crit (short for critique, also called a jury or review) is a session where you present your project to tutors and invited critics, who then question and discuss it. It is not an exam with right answers; it is a design conversation. The panel is testing your thinking as much as your drawings — how you arrived at decisions, whether you understand the consequences, and how you respond when an idea is pushed. Understanding this reframes the whole event: you are not being judged as a person, your project is being interrogated so it can improve.</p>

  <h2>How the format works</h2>
  <p>Most crits follow a rhythm: you pin up your work, present for a few minutes, and then take questions and comments. There may be several students in the room, sometimes presenting one after another. Critics range from your own tutors to external architects you have never met, and their styles vary from gentle to blunt. Knowing the running order and how long you have is half the preparation, so ask your tutor in advance what to expect.</p>
  <div class="callout"><strong>Tip.</strong> Pin up early and stand back to read your own wall as a stranger would. If the story is not clear from across the room, rearrange the sheets before anyone arrives.</div>

  <h2>Presenting well</h2>
  <p>Open with the essence of your project in one or two sentences — the problem you set yourself and your response — before diving into detail. Walk the panel through a clear narrative: site and brief, concept, then how the design develops and resolves. Point to your drawings as you speak so words and images reinforce each other. Speak a little slower than feels natural, make eye contact, and resist the urge to apologise for unfinished work; present what you have with conviction. Practising aloud, even once, transforms how you come across.</p>

  <h2>Handling criticism</h2>
  <p>When comments come, listen fully before responding, and take a breath rather than defending on reflex. Some feedback will be fair, some will be a critic thinking out loud, and some you will disagree with — all of it is useful. It is completely acceptable to say &ldquo;that&rsquo;s a good point, I hadn&rsquo;t considered it&rdquo;, and it is equally fine to explain your reasoning where you made a deliberate choice. What panels respect is a designer who can hold a position and also stay open. Write comments down as they come; you will not remember them afterwards, and they are the raw material for your next iteration.</p>
  <div class="callout warn"><strong>Watch out.</strong> Do not argue to win. Turning the crit into a contest reads as defensiveness and shuts down the very feedback you came for. Absorb now, evaluate later.</div>

  <h2>Managing your time</h2>
  <p>Two clocks matter: the weeks before and the minutes during. In the run-up, plan backwards from the deadline and protect time for printing and pinning up, which always takes longer than expected — the all-nighter is a rite of passage that ruins your delivery, not a badge of honour. On the day, rehearse to fit the slot; a crisp presentation that leaves room for discussion beats a rambling one that gets cut off. Have your key points ready so that if you are stopped early, the most important idea has already been said.</p>

  <h2>Looking after yourself</h2>
  <p>Crits are emotionally intense, and it is normal to feel exposed. Separate the critique of the work from your worth as a person — the two are not the same, however personal a hard review can feel. Eat and sleep before the day rather than sacrificing both to the model; you present better rested than exhausted. Afterwards, resist judging yourself in the raw hour that follows; wait a day, reread your notes with a clear head, and you will usually find the feedback was more constructive than it felt in the room. Talk to classmates, because everyone experiences the same nerves, and lean on tutors and student support if crits are affecting your wellbeing.</p>

  <h2>After the crit</h2>
  <p>The crit is the beginning of the next stage, not the end of this one. Within a day or two, sort your notes into changes you agree with, points to investigate, and comments to set aside, then fold the useful ones into your development. Over a degree you will sit through dozens of these, and each one makes the next less frightening — the students who improve fastest are simply the ones who treat every crit as data rather than a verdict.</p>
